-- title:
--   Rational points of the Eisenstein quotient are torsion
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a natural number assumed prime, and let `hcomm` assert that the operators `heckeOperatorBar p ℓ`, $\ell$ ranging over the primes, commute pairwise on $J_0(p)$, here modelled as `JZero p`, the degree-zero divisor class group `Pic0` of the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the modular function field of level $p$; `heckeModuleBar p` is the resulting module structure for `HeckeAlg` $= \mathbb{Z}[T_\ell : \ell$ prime$]$. Write $R$ for the set `eisensteinQuotientRational p (heckeModuleBar p)`, the image under `eisensteinQuotientMk` of those $x \in$ `JZero p` with $\sigma \cdot x - x$ in the submodule `eisensteinKernel (JZero p) (eisensteinIdeal p) • ⊤` for every $\sigma \in \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$, where `eisensteinIdeal p` is the kernel of the evaluation of `HeckeAlg` at $T_\ell \mapsto 1$ for $\ell \mid p$ and $T_\ell \mapsto 1+\ell$ otherwise. Put $n = |p-1| / \gcd(p-1,12)$ (natural division). Assume: (`hfgc`) the additive subgroup generated by $R$ is finitely generated; (`hA`) for every prime $q \mid n$ the filtration $\mathfrak{P}_q^m \cdot \operatorname{span}_{\mathrm{HeckeAlg}} R$ is eventually constant, where $\mathfrak{P}_q =$ `eisensteinMaximalIdeal p q` is the preimage of $(q) \subseteq \mathbb{Z}$ under that evaluation map; (`hB`) any $z$ in the subgroup generated by $R$ for which, for every prime $q$, some $s \notin \mathfrak{P}_q$ has $s \cdot z$ of finite additive order, itself has finite additive order; (`hC`) some $t$ in `eisensteinIdeal p` acts on `JZero p` as $k$ times the identity for a nonzero integer $k$ with $|k| \mid n$. Conclusion: every $z \in R$ satisfies $n' \cdot z = 0$ for some $n' > 0$.
--
--   This is the torsion half of Mazur's Theorem III.3.1 for the Eisenstein quotient: the rational points of $\tilde{J}$ are of finite order, assembled from per-prime stabilisation of the $\mathfrak{P}_q$-adic filtration, a local-to-global torsion criterion, and the existence of an element of the Eisenstein ideal acting as an integer dividing the numerator of $(p-1)/12$. It feeds [`ModularCurve.eisensteinQuotientRational_isTorsion_heckeModuleBar`](thm.html#ModularCurve.eisensteinQuotientRational_isTorsion_heckeModuleBar), and thence the finiteness of $\tilde{J}(\mathbb{Q})$ used in Mazur's third step.
